<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Courland Gambia</h1><p class="st-meta">1651 CE – 1661 CE · capital Fort Jacob</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Colony of the small Baltic Duchy of Courland on an island in the Gambia River, part of Duke Jacob Kettler's bold overseas venture.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-kettler">Duke Jacob Kettler</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia">Duchy of Courland and Semigallia</a>, who built a merchant fleet, acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunta-kinteh-island">St Andrew's Island</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> River from local rulers in 1651. His men built Fort Jacob on the island and trading posts on the river banks.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony was a small garrisoned trading station dealing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, gold, beeswax, hides and enslaved people. It depended on supplies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> and on agreements with the kings of Kombo and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-niumi">Niumi</a>. Its distance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the small size of Courland made it hard to sustain.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>While <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-kettler">Duke Jacob</a> was held prisoner by the Swedes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> seized the fort in 1659, and in 1661 the English took it and renamed it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunta-kinteh-island">James Island</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-england">Duke of York</a>. The island, now Kunta Kinteh Island, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">The Gambia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1651 CE – 1661 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.56%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1651 CE – 1661 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fort Jacob</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jacob Kettler</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity, Islam, African traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Mandinka</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>The Gambia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>St Andrew's Island colony, Courland Gambia colony</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1656&amp;focus=courland-gambia" aria-label="Courland Gambia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4269 1670 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Courland Gambia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5513 509h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4589 1926h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4589 1926h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day The Gambia. <a href="/atlas?year=1656&amp;focus=courland-gambia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

